        "en" to GemDetails(
            "Conch pearl is a non-nacreous natural pearl produced by the queen conch (Aliger gigas, formerly Strombus gigas), a large marine gastropod of the Caribbean. It forms naturally, without human intervention, when an irritant enters the mollusc's mantle, which coats it in concentric layers of calcium carbonate similar to the porcelain-like interior of its shell. Fewer than one conch in ten thousand produces one, and fewer than one in a hundred of those is of gem quality. No commercial culturing has succeeded to date, which makes it rare and precious. Its salmon-pink colour comes from carotenoid pigments.",
            "Biogenic material: aragonite (orthorhombic) in fibres and crossed lamellae, with a little calcite and organic matter. Non-nacreous, porcelain-like structure. Hardness 3.5 to 5; specific gravity 2.83 to 2.86; porcelaneous to silky lustre; opaque. Its hallmark is the “flame”, a shimmering wavy pattern caused by the fibrous structure, the more prized the sharper it is. Shapes are often oval or baroque. The colours fade in intense, prolonged light; store away from light.",
            "The Caribbean: the Bahamas, the Turks and Caicos Islands and, more widely, the Caribbean Sea from Bermuda to the north coast of South America, where pearls are found by conch fishermen, whose catch is eaten. The queen conch has been listed in CITES Appendix II since 1992: international trade, including in shells and pearls, requires permits, and fishing is regulated or suspended in several countries.",
            "A stone of femininity, gentleness and joy, said to soothe the emotions, open the heart and help express feelings. It is associated with the heart and sacral chakras and the sign of Cancer. Avoid prolonged bright light, acids, perfumes and knocks; wipe with a soft cloth; cleanse with sound; recharge in moonlight."
